---
to: "lib/{{ app_module | lower }}_web/mcp_descriptor.ex"
for_each: "spec"
sparql:
  spec: queries/surface.rq
  caps: queries/capabilities.rq
---
{# Real implementation of v2030:MCPCapabilityDescriptor (docs/ontology/
   vision-2030.ttl, this session) -- the concrete module the beam4pm
   requirements pass named as the missing gap for beam4pm_discovery.ex's
   real pure functions: "add Beam4pm.McpDescriptor with describe/1 or a
   static registry capabilities/0". Generalized here to any consumer
   generating from an ema:CapabilitySurface, not beam4pm-specific. #}
defmodule {{ app_module }}Web.McpDescriptor do
  @moduledoc """
  Per-capability metadata for `{{ app_module }}`'s generated MCP/A2A
  surface: observation scope, mutation scope, authority requirement,
  reversibility, cost, and receipt semantics -- the real implementation
  of v2030:MCPCapabilityDescriptor for this surface's capability list.

  A caller (an MCP client, an A2A peer, or this app's own router/plug)
  can check `requires_authority?/1` or `mutation_scope/1` BEFORE invoking
  a capability, rather than discovering its authority requirement only
  from a runtime refusal.
  """

  {# Atom key quoting: real defect caught by the actual `mix compile
     --warnings-as-errors` gate ggen_igniter.sync runs on every sync --
     "hddl-plan" as a bare `hddl-plan:` key is invalid Elixir (hyphen);
     unconditionally quoting every id then trips Elixir's OWN unnecessary-
     quote warning under --warnings-as-errors for plain ids like
     "checkout". This Tera `is containing("-")` check covers the one real
     hazard class observed (hyphenated ids); the EEx sibling template
     (priv/ggen_igniter/mcp_a2a/templates/mcp_descriptor.eex in a
     consuming repo) uses a full identifier regex, a narrower-but-
     sufficient check here since Tera's built-in test set has no regex. #}
  @capabilities %{
{% for c in caps %}    {% if c.capability_id is containing("-") %}:"{{ c.capability_id }}"{% else %}:{{ c.capability_id }}{% endif %} => %{
      name: "{{ c.capability_name }}",
      description: "{{ c.capability_description }}",
      observation_scope: :{{ c.observation_scope }},
      mutation_scope: :{{ c.mutation_scope }},
      requires_authority: {{ c.requires_authority }},
      reversible: {{ c.is_reversible }},
      cost_estimate: {% if c.cost_estimate %}"{{ c.cost_estimate }}"{% else %}nil{% endif %},

      receipt_semantics: :{{ c.receipt_semantics }},
      ash_action: {% if c.capability_ash_action %}"{{ c.capability_ash_action }}"{% else %}nil{% endif %}

    }{% if not loop.last %},
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}  }

  @doc "Full descriptor list, e.g. for an MCP `tools/list` or A2A agent-card response."
  @spec capabilities() :: %{atom() => map()}
  def capabilities, do: @capabilities

  @doc "Descriptor for one capability id, or `:error` if unregistered."
  @spec describe(atom()) :: {:ok, map()} | :error
  def describe(capability_id), do: Map.fetch(@capabilities, capability_id)

  @doc """
  Real authority check per capability, per this ecosystem's own doctrine
  (xaas CLAUDE.md's "Consequential DO" section, ex4pm's BRCE.execute/5):
  a `:consequential` mutation_scope with `requires_authority: true` must
  never be callable without an explicit authority context. This function
  does not itself GRANT authority -- it only reports whether the
  capability demands one, so a caller can enforce the fence before
  dispatch, consistent with the SELECT/CONSTRUCT/DO separation this
  ecosystem's own vision-2030.ttl models as odrl:Permission/Prohibition.
  """
  @spec requires_authority?(atom()) :: boolean()
  def requires_authority?(capability_id) do
    case describe(capability_id) do
      {:ok, %{requires_authority: r}} -> r
      :error -> true
    end
  end
end
